  "attack_progression": [
    {"step": 1, "tactic": "Defense Evasion / Masquerading (Renamed Binary in User Path)", "technique": "T1036.005", "what_happened": "At 2026-12-05 10:08:18 CET, Sysmon Event 1 on WS-FIN-19 (10.30.6.51) logged image=C:\\Users\\sarah.n\\AppData\\Roaming\\svchost32.exe, parentImage=explorer.exe, user=CORP\\sarah.n, signed=false, hash=a9d4c2b1f9e7c8d3a4b5c6d7e8f9a0b1c2d3e4f5a6b7c8d9e0f1a2b3c4d5e6f7. The name 'svchost32.exe' is a deliberate masquerade — it impersonates the legitimate Service Host process svchost.exe (which lives in C:\\Windows\\System32, never in a user profile, and is ALWAYS Microsoft-signed). The '32' suffix is a tool default that fools casual eyeballing of process lists. parentImage=explorer.exe = the binary was launched by the user via a desktop / start-menu / Explorer click (likely from a phishing attachment recently extracted to AppData\\Roaming). User sarah.n is in FINANCE — high-value target for credential and wallet capture.", "chain_reason": "Sysmon Event 1 with image path matching %AppData%\\Roaming\\svchost*.exe + signed=false + parentImage=explorer.exe is a categorical masquerading indicator (real svchost.exe is in C:\\Windows\\System32 and Microsoft-signed). The user-writable AppData\\Roaming path is the standard low-privilege persistence drop location.", "first_seen": "2026-12-05T10:08:18+0100"},
    {"step": 2, "tactic": "Execution (Implant Live)", "technique": "T1204.002", "what_happened": "At 10:11:02 (2 minutes 44 seconds after the first drop / launch), Sysmon Event 1 confirms svchost32.exe is RUNNING — process tree alive, ready to begin its collection phase. The 2m44s warm-up gap is consistent with implant sandbox-evasion / sleep-loop before activating malicious behavior.", "chain_reason": "Sysmon Event 1 with same image=svchost32.exe + same user=CORP\\sarah.n + same agent=WS-FIN-19 = continuity of execution.", "first_seen": "2026-12-05T10:11:02+0100"},
    {"step": 3, "tactic": "Collection (Clipboard Polling — SMOKING GUN)", "technique": "T1115", "what_happened": "At 10:11:05 (3 seconds after step 2), EDR API_CALL telemetry on WS-FIN-19 fired: process=svchost32.exe function=GetClipboardData. At 10:11:12 (7 seconds later), a SECOND GetClipboardData call from the same process. This 7-second interval is the canonical clipboard-scraper polling pattern (T1115 Clipboard Data) — the implant calls GetClipboardData on a timer to harvest whatever the user copies. Legitimate processes that call GetClipboardData on a tight repeating timer are essentially zero outside of clipboard managers (Ditto, ClipClip, 1Password copy-paste flows) — and none of those are named 'svchost32.exe' in a user AppData path.", "chain_reason": "EDR API_CALL telemetry: process=svchost32.exe + function=GetClipboardData repeating at ~7s interval. The function name is a Windows API (user32.dll!GetClipboardData), not an attacker-specific string — it is the textbook T1115 primitive.", "first_seen": "2026-12-05T10:11:05+0100"},
    {"step": 4, "tactic": "Collection (Clipboard Hook Installed)", "technique": "T1115 + T1056.004", "what_happened": "At 10:11:18 (6 seconds after the second GetClipboardData call), EDR API_CALL: process=svchost32.exe function=SetWindowsHookEx. This installs a Windows clipboard / shell hook so that the implant can be NOTIFIED on every clipboard change (WM_CLIPBOARDUPDATE / WH_GETMESSAGE) instead of polling — drastically more efficient and lower-noise than a 7-second polling loop. The combination 'polling THEN hook' suggests the implant first verified clipboard access via polling then upgraded to event-driven capture for stealth.", "chain_reason": "EDR API_CALL: process=svchost32.exe + function=SetWindowsHookEx. SetWindowsHookEx from a non-Microsoft-signed user-AppData process is universally suspicious; combined with prior GetClipboardData calls = clipboard-monitor hook installation.", "first_seen": "2026-12-05T10:11:18+0100"},
    {"step": 5, "tactic": "Command & Control + Exfiltration (First POST)", "technique": "T1071.001 + T1041", "what_happened": "At 10:11:20 (2 seconds after the hook installation), Sysmon Event 3 on WS-FIN-19: svchost32.exe initiated outbound HTTPS POST 2.4KB to 185.199.221.77:443. The 2-second latency from hook-install to first exfil POST = the implant immediately exfiltrated whatever was already in the clipboard at hook-installation time (plus any session/host fingerprint header). The destination 185.199.221.77 is hosted in NL ALPHA-NET and cataloged as a C&C + Phishing node.", "chain_reason": "Sysmon Event 3: image=svchost32.exe + destination IP external + immediately after SetWindowsHookEx = exfil-of-initial-clipboard-snapshot signature.", "first_seen": "2026-12-05T10:11:20+0100"},
    {"step": 6, "tactic": "Collection (Continued Polling)", "technique": "T1115", "what_happened": "At 10:12:42 (1m22s after the hook + first exfil), EDR API_CALL telemetry shows GetClipboardData calls CONTINUE at ~7-second intervals. The implant is running BOTH the polling loop and the hook in parallel — the polling acts as a fallback / redundancy check in case the hook is detached. Every clipboard copy the user performs is now captured.", "chain_reason": "EDR API_CALL: continued GetClipboardData every ~7s = sustained polling. Operationally important: this is the symptom that lets us distinguish a clipboard-scraper from a one-off clipboard read (legitimate apps GetClipboardData once on demand; T1115 implants poll).", "first_seen": "2026-12-05T10:12:42+0100"},
    {"step": 7, "tactic": "Exfiltration (Periodic POST cadence)", "technique": "T1041 + T1029", "what_happened": "At 10:13:18 (1m58s after the first POST), Sysmon Event 3 logged a SECOND outbound HTTPS POST of 1.8KB from svchost32.exe to the same 185.199.221.77:443. Two POSTs in <2 minutes confirm a PERIODIC exfiltration timer (likely ~60-120s with jitter). Each POST carries the clipboard captures accumulated since the last exfil — wallet addresses copied by the user, password-manager copies of credentials, internal document snippets, account numbers. CRYPTO-CLIPPER risk: many T1115 implants ALSO modify the clipboard on-the-fly (SetClipboardData) to swap copied cryptocurrency wallet addresses with the attacker's address before the user pastes — leading to silent theft on paste.", "chain_reason": "Sysmon Event 3: second POST to same destination, same image. Two POSTs <2min apart = periodic timer confirmed. The risk of integrated clipper-attack behavior (wallet-address swap) is inferred from the function profile (T1115 polling + hook + external POST loop = clipper toolkit signature).", "first_seen": "2026-12-05T10:13:18+0100"}
